## Lifecycle and Ownership

The provider must connect as a superuser or a member of `sys:secadmin`, the only identities that can create masking
policies and read `SVV_MASKING_POLICY`. Statements run in `database`, so the policy name stays unqualified.

`expression` updates in place with `ALTER MASKING POLICY`, which keeps the policy's attachments. `ALTER` cannot change
the input columns or their types, so changing an input column's name or type replaces the policy; Redshift refuses to
drop a policy that is still attached, so replace or remove its attachments first. Deletion never cascades.

Redshift stores the expression in its own rendering, for example with the input columns qualified. State keeps the
configured `expression` while the catalog still matches `definition_fingerprint`, the hash recorded at apply time.
When the policy is altered outside Terraform, refresh shows the catalog text in `expression` and the next apply restores
the configured one. Equivalent input types, such as `TEXT` and `VARCHAR(256)`, do not count as a change, neither on
refresh nor in a plan: switching between them updates the state in place without running SQL. A type without length
gets the length Redshift gives a column, so `VARCHAR` means `VARCHAR(256)`. The routine pseudo-types `ANYELEMENT` and
`REFCURSOR` are rejected, because no column has them.

## Import

In Terraform 1.5 and later, use an `import` block with a JSON identity:

{{ tffile "examples/resources/redshift_masking_policy/import-by-string-id.tf" }}

Alternatively, use `terraform import`:

{{ codefile "shell" "examples/resources/redshift_masking_policy/import.sh" }}

Import reads the `input_column` blocks and `expression` from the catalog in Redshift's spelling, for example
`character varying(256)`. The first plan therefore shows an in-place update of `expression` unless the configuration
repeats the catalog text, and of `input_column` when the configuration spells an input type differently; neither
replaces the policy, and only the `expression` update runs `ALTER MASKING POLICY`.
